I tested the redirect finding with two local test servers, and it's real: yoyo sends the API key and the whole request to whatever address a redirect points at. That's the strongest new item in today's assessment.

What I ran: server A, on one local port, answers every request with a 307 redirect to server B on another port; server B records what it receives. I pointed yoyo at A with a fake key (canary-not-a-key), using the built binary for the current HEAD (4ae43f30). No real host was contacted.

| Provider | What server B got |
|---|---|
| anthropic | the key in x-api-key, plus the full ~10.5 KB request (system prompt and user prompt), on all 6 retry attempts |
| custom (key sent as Authorization: Bearer) | no key, because reqwest removes Authorization when a redirect changes host; the full ~10.8 KB request still arrived |

So for providers that send the key in their own header (x-api-key, and by the code, Azure's api-key), a redirecting base_url or proxy gets both the key and the prompt. For bearer-key providers it gets the prompt only.

The likely fix is in yoagent: its model requests need the same-origin redirect rule its MCP HTTP transport already has, backed by a yoyo test using a recording server like tests/provider_key_canary.rs. No issue exists for this in either repo yet; it should be filed before any task starts on it.

